      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn what the book means by a surd.\n\n√7 is not a tidy rational number, so it stays as a root.\n\n√9 is different: it equals 3 exactly.\n\nThe chapter calls an irrational nth root of a rational number a surd, and n is its order.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "concept_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "concept_1": {
      "id": "concept_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The order is the root being taken.\n\n√7 has order 2.\n∛5 has order 3.\n⁴√11 has order 4.\n\nThe chapter writes this in general as the nth root of a.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
